{"batchcomplete":true,"query":{"pages":[{"pageid":72478180,"ns":0,"title":"Garth Ehrlich","revisions":[{"revid":1379994366,"parentid":1370418144,"minor":true,"timestamp":"2026-10-13T03:40:21Z","size":33292,"sha1":"2c1e8a3a8327708feb347bcf0cc6fd6e2b86d354","slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","content":"{{Short description|American molecular biologist}}\n{{Infobox academic\n| name         = Garth David Ehrlich\n| image        = \n| occupation   = [[Molecular biology|Molecular biologist]], [[Genomics|genomic]] scientist, academic, and author\n| title        = \n| awards       = <!--notable national-level awards only-->\n| website      = \n| education    = BA, and PhD\n| alma_mater   = [[Alfred University]]<br />[[Syracuse University]]\n| thesis_title = \n| thesis_url   = \n| thesis_year  = \n| institutions = [[Drexel University College of Medicine]] (DUCoM)\n}}\n'''Garth David Ehrlich''' is a [[Molecular biology|molecular biologist]], [[Genomics|genomic]] scientist, academic, and author who is most known for his development of the distributed genome hypothesis and bringing the [[biofilm]] paradigm to the field of chronic mucosal bacterial diseases. He is a professor of [[Microbiology]] and [[Immunology]], and Otolaryngology-Head and Neck Surgery at [[Drexel University]]. He is also the founder and executive director of three Research Centers of Excellence: the Center for Genomic Sciences (CGS); the Center for Advanced Microbial Processing (CAMP); and the Center for Surgical Infections and Biofilms.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.meduniwien.ac.at/hp/en/cepii/news/news-daten/events/single-view/?tx_ttnews%5Btt_news%5D=3021&tx_ttnews%5Bpointer%5D=6&cHash=7fd985b1d641b9b4bd128adfa958cdb3|title=Prof. Garth Ehrlich, MD: \"Biofilms and Modeling Chronic Bacterial Pathogenesis\"|website=www.meduniwien.ac.at}}</ref> In addition, he serves as the executive director of the Genomics Core Facility and the director of Molecular Pathology within Drexel Medicine Diagnostics and the [[Sidney Kimmel]] Cancer Center's Meta-omics Core Facility.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://skcc-pa.ilabsolutions.com/service_center/show_external/4219?name=metaomics-core-facility#:~:text=The+Sidney+Kimmel+Cancer+Center+Meta-omics+Core+Facility,Diseases+(IMMID)+at+Drexel+University+College+of+Medicine.|title=iLab Organizer :: MetaOmics Core Facility|website=skcc-pa.ilabsolutions.com}}</ref>\n\nEhrlich has authored two books entitled, ''PCR-Based Diagnostics in Infectious Disease'', and ''Culture-Negative Orthopedic Biofilm Infections''. His research interests revolve around the field of molecular pathogenesis with a particular focus on chronic infectious diseases, human genetic susceptibility, and resistance to infections by employing comparative genomic technologies.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.researchgate.net/profile/Garth-Ehrlich|title=Garth D Ehrlich – Research Gate Profile}}</ref>\n\nEhrlich is an elected Fellow of the [[American Association for the Advancement of Science]],<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.aaas.org/news/aaas-fellow-class-2014-announced|title=AAAS Fellow Class of 2014 Announced &#124; American Association for the Advancement of Science (AAAS)|website=www.aaas.org}}</ref> the American Academy of Microbiology,<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://asm.org/Press-Releases/2022/Feb-2022/65-Fellows-Elected-into-the-American-Academy-of-Mi|title=65 Fellows Elected into the American Academy of Microbiology|website=ASM.org}}</ref> and the Editor-in-Chief ''emeritus'' of, ''[[Genetic Testing and Molecular Biomarkers]]''.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://home.liebertpub.com/publications/genetic-testing-and-molecular-biomarkers/18/editorial-board|title=Genetic Testing and Molecular Biomarkers &#124; Mary Ann Liebert, Inc., publishers|website=home.liebertpub.com}}</ref> He is also the Associate Editor of ''BMC Ecology and Evolution'',<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://bmcecolevol.biomedcentral.com/about/editorial-board|title=BMC Ecology and Evolution|website=BioMed Central}}</ref> and ''Genes''.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.mdpi.com/journal/genes/editors|title=Genes|website=www.mdpi.com}}</ref> As of 2022, he is the chair of the scientific advisory board of Keystone Bio which is a bio-technology company developing biologicals to combat systemic inflammatory disease.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.bloomberg.com/press-releases/2022-11-09/keystone-bio-adds-significant-scientific-leadership-with-appointment-of-medical-advisor-and-new-scientific-advisory-board|title=Keystone Bio Adds Significant Scientific Leadership with Appointment of Medical Advisor and New Scientific Advisory Board|newspaper=Bloomberg.com |date=November 9, 2022}}</ref>\n\n==Education==\nEhrlich graduated with a [[Bachelor of Arts]] in biology from [[Alfred University]] in 1977. He, then enrolled at [[Syracuse University]] for a Ph.D. in molecular biology and graduated in 1987 during which time he was a member of the team that first applied [[Polymerase chain reaction|PCR]] to the detection of low copy number infectious agents.<ref name=\"auto\">{{Cite journal|title=Identification of human immunodeficiency virus sequences by using in vitro enzymatic amplification and oligomer cleavage detection|first1=S.|last1=Kwok|first2=D. H.|last2=Mack|first3=K. B.|last3=Mullis|first4=B.|last4=Poiesz|first5=G.|last5=Ehrlich|first6=D.|last6=Blair|first7=A.|last7=Friedman-Kien|first8=J. J.|last8=Sninsky|date=May 12, 1987|journal=Journal of Virology|volume=61|issue=5|pages=1690–1694|doi=10.1128/JVI.61.5.1690-1694.1987|pmid=2437321|pmc=254157 }}</ref> Subsequently, he undertook a postdoctoral fellowship in human retrovirology at [[State University of New York Upstate Medical University|SUNY Upstate Medical Center]] where he demonstrated that [[Human T-lymphotropic virus 1|HTLV-I]] was associated with a [[demyelinating disease]] of the [[central nervous system]].<ref name=mnm>{{Cite web|url=https://iladef.org/about-us/officers-and-directors/|title=Officers and Directors|website=ILADEF}}</ref>\n\n==Career==\nEhrlich has held many academic appointments throughout his career. He was formerly an assistant professor and then an associate professor with tenure at the [[University of Pittsburgh]] between 1990 and 1997.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.tribliveoffers.com/|title=Welcome|website=www.tribliveoffers.com}}</ref> Since 1997 he has served as the director of research in the Department of Otolaryngology at the Drexel University College of Medicine (DUCoM).<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://drexel.edu/medicine/about/departments/otolaryngology/faculty/|title=Faculty: Department of Otolaryngology|date=December 5, 2022|website=College of Medicine}}</ref>\n\nEhrlich held an appointment as a Technical Specialist at the State University of New York Research Foundation between 1984 and 1988. From 1997 till 2013, he held an appointment as the managing director at the [[Allegheny General Hospital]]/Allegheny Singer Research Institute. He is the president of the International Lyme and Associated Diseases Educational Foundation (ILADEF),<ref name=mnm/> and has served as a consultant for IMS and Teltech.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://lifeboat.com/ex/bios.garth.d.ehrlich|title=Lifeboat Foundation Bios: Dr. Garth D. Ehrlich|website=lifeboat.com}}</ref> He directs the Center for Genomic Sciences (CGS), and the Center for Advanced Microbial Processing (CAMP), the Center for Surgical Infections and Biofilms, and Genomics Core Facility at the Drexel University College of Medicine as the executive director. Additionally, he directs the Meta-Omics Core Facility at the Sidney Kimmel Cancer Center, which is a [[National Cancer Institute]] designated Cancer Center that encompasses [[Thomas Jefferson University]] and Drexel University.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://drexel.edu/medicine/faculty/profiles/garth-ehrlich/|title=Garth Ehrlich, PhD: Microbiology & Immunology|date=February 24, 2021|website=College of Medicine}}</ref>\n\n==Research==\nEhrlich's research works span the fields of [[molecular diagnostics]], [[genomics]], [[molecular medicine]], and [[evolutionary biology]] with particular emphases on chronic bacterial pathogenesis, comparative bacterial genomics, and [[human genetics]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=_BTZcJAAAAAJ&hl=en|title=Garth D. Ehrlich, Ph.D., F.A.A.A.S., F.A.A.M.|website=scholar.google.com}}</ref>\n\n===Biofilm, and identification of anti-biofilm drugs===\nEhrlich's research on the etiology of chronic middle-ear disease ([[otitis media]]) in the early 1990s in which he exploited the potential of the emergent field of molecular diagnostics led to his development of the biofilm paradigm of chronic infectious diseases.<ref>{{Cite journal|title=Biofilms and Chronic Infections|first1=Randall D.|last1=Wolcott|first2=Garth D.|last2=Ehrlich|date=June 11, 2008|journal=JAMA|volume=299|issue=22|pages=2682–2684|doi=10.1001/jama.299.22.2682|pmid=18544729 |bibcode=2008JAMA..299.2682W }}</ref>\n\nEhrlich's biofilm work began with chronic otitis media with effusion (COME) and soon encompassed other chronic respiratory mucosal diseases including [[sinusitis]]; [[adenoiditis]]; [[pharyngitis]]; and [[chronic obstructive pulmonary disease]] (COPD). Using the chinchilla ''(Chinchilla lanigera)'' experimental model of COME his research supported the hypothesis that biofilm formation was a key driver in its pathogenesis.<ref>{{cite journal|title=Mucosal Biofilm Formation on Middle-Ear Mucosa in the Chinchilla Model of Otitis Media|year=2002 |doi=10.1001/jama.287.13.1710 |last1=Ehrlich |first1=Garth D. |last2=Veeh |first2=R. |last3=Wang |first3=X. |last4=Costerton |first4=J. W. |last5=Hayes |first5=J. D. |last6=Hu |first6=F. Z. |last7=Daigle |first7=B. J. |last8=Ehrlich |first8=M. D. |last9=Post |first9=J. C. |journal=JAMA |volume=287 |issue=13 |pages=1710–1715 |pmid=11926896 |doi-access=free }}</ref> Subsequently, he led a team that directly demonstrated the formation of bacterial biofilms on the middle-ear mucosa of children who had failed multiple rounds of antibiotic therapy, yet still suffered from either COME or recurrent OM.<ref>{{cite journal|title=Direct Detection of Bacterial Biofilms on the Middle-Ear Mucosa of Children With Chronic Otitis Media|year=2006 |doi=10.1001/jama.296.2.202 |last1=Hall-Stoodley |first1=Luanne |last2=Hu |first2=Fen Ze |last3=Gieseke |first3=Armin |last4=Nistico |first4=Laura |last5=Nguyen |first5=Duc |last6=Hayes |first6=Jay |last7=Forbes |first7=Michael |last8=Greenberg |first8=David P. |last9=Dice |first9=Bethany |last10=Burrows |first10=Amy |last11=Wackym |first11=P. Ashley |last12=Stoodley |first12=Paul |last13=Post |first13=J. Christopher |last14=Ehrlich |first14=Garth D. |last15=Kerschner |first15=Joseph E. |journal=JAMA |volume=296 |issue=2 |pages=202–211 |pmid=16835426 |pmc=1885379 }}</ref>\n\nEhrlich then developed a metabolic framework for understanding biofilm biology which he subsequently used for the development of a science for the treatment and management of infectious diseases.<ref>{{Cite journal|title=The application of biofilm science to the study and control of chronic bacterial infections|first1=William|last1=Costerton|first2=Richard|last2=Veeh|first3=Mark|last3=Shirtliff|first4=Mark|last4=Pasmore|first5=Christopher|last5=Post|first6=Garth|last6=Ehrlich|date=November 15, 2003|journal=The Journal of Clinical Investigation|volume=112|issue=10|pages=1466–1477|doi=10.1172/JCI20365|pmid=14617746|pmc=259139 }}</ref>\n\nWhile examining the role of biofilms in multiple otolaryngologic infections, Ehrlich concluded that understanding their physiology would be key to the formulation of effective treatment strategies<ref>{{Cite journal|url=https://journals.lww.com/co-otolaryngology/Abstract/2004/06000/The_role_of_biofilms_in_otolaryngologic_infections.7.aspx|title=The role of biofilms in otolaryngologic infections|first1=J. Christopher|last1=Post|first2=Paul|last2=Stoodley|first3=Luanne|last3=Hall–Stoodley|first4=Garth D.|last4=Ehrlich|date=June 12, 2004|journal=Current Opinion in Otolaryngology & Head and Neck Surgery|volume=12|issue=3|pages=185–190|doi=10.1097/01.moo.0000124936.46948.6a|pmid=15167027 |s2cid=43730703 |url-access=subscription}}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal|url=https://journals.lww.com/co-otolaryngology/Abstract/2007/10000/The_role_of_biofilms_in_otolaryngologic.11.aspx|title=The role of biofilms in otolaryngologic infections: update 2007|first1=J. Christopher|last1=Post|first2=N. Luisa|last2=Hiller|first3=Laura|last3=Nistico|first4=Paul|last4=Stoodley|first5=Garth D.|last5=Ehrlich|date=October 12, 2007|journal=Current Opinion in Otolaryngology & Head and Neck Surgery|volume=15|issue=5|pages=347–351|doi=10.1097/MOO.0b013e3282b97327|pmid=17823552 |s2cid=21171594 |url-access=subscription}}</ref> as biofilm bacteria are resistant to classical antimicrobial therapies. With this focus on developing methods for ameliorating chronic bacterial infections, he led a research team to identify novel bacterial treatment targets and to find small molecular inhibitors that could serve as anti-biofilm drugs leading to the creation of the biotech company ASR BIO Inc. This work built upon his realization that biofilm resistance to antibiotics was not primarily due to issues of penetration, but rather, as he illustrated, due to metabolic quiescence attributable to oxygen limitation and a lack of fermentable substrates as he demonstrated using ''Pseudomonas aeruginosa'' as a model biofilm organism.<ref>{{Cite journal|title=Arginine or Nitrate Enhances Antibiotic Susceptibility of Pseudomonas aeruginosa in Biofilms|first1=Giorgia|last1=Borriello|first2=Lee|last2=Richards|first3=Garth D.|last3=Ehrlich|first4=Philip S.|last4=Stewart|date=January 12, 2006|journal=Antimicrobial Agents and Chemotherapy|volume=50|issue=1|pages=382–384|doi=10.1128/AAC.50.1.382-384.2006|pmid=16377718|pmc=1346784}}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal|title=Oxygen Limitation Contributes to Antibiotic Tolerance of Pseudomonas aeruginosa in Biofilms|first1=Giorgia|last1=Borriello|first2=Erin|last2=Werner|first3=Frank|last3=Roe|first4=Aana M.|last4=Kim|first5=Garth D.|last5=Ehrlich|first6=Philip S.|last6=Stewart|date=July 12, 2004|journal=Antimicrobial Agents and Chemotherapy|volume=48|issue=7|pages=2659–2664|doi=10.1128/AAC.48.7.2659-2664.2004|pmid=15215123|pmc=434183}}</ref> This anti-biofilm research program has resulted in multiple patent applications and employs a hybrid ''in silico'' and laboratory pipeline for the identification and characterization of small inhibitor molecules which can be used in combination with traditional antibiotics for the treatment of biofilm infection by targeting the bacterial [[stringent response]].<ref>{{Cite journal|title=The Development of a Pipeline for the Identification and Validation of Small-Molecule RelA Inhibitors for Use as Anti-Biofilm Drugs|first1=Donald C.|last1=Hall|first2=Jarosław E.|last2=Król|first3=John P.|last3=Cahill|first4=Hai-Feng|last4=Ji|first5=Garth D.|last5=Ehrlich|date=September 12, 2020|journal=Microorganisms|volume=8|issue=9|page=1310|doi=10.3390/microorganisms8091310|pmid=32872142 |pmc=7563162 |doi-access=free }}</ref> In another research study, his team reported that with the controlled use of salicylic acid-releasing polymers, the biofilm formation can be hindered on plastic catheters.<ref>{{Cite journal|url=https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1742706112000487|title=Salicylic acid-releasing polyurethane acrylate polymers as anti-biofilm urological catheter coatings|first1=Paul J.|last1=Nowatzki|first2=Richard R.|last2=Koepsel|first3=Paul|last3=Stoodley|first4=Ke|last4=Min|first5=Alan|last5=Harper|first6=Hironobu|last6=Murata|first7=Joseph|last7=Donfack|first8=Edwin R.|last8=Hortelano|first9=Garth D.|last9=Ehrlich|first10=Alan J.|last10=Russell|date=May 1, 2012|journal=Acta Biomaterialia|volume=8|issue=5|pages=1869–1880|doi=10.1016/j.actbio.2012.01.032|pmid=22342353 |url-access=subscription}}</ref> In addition, he evaluated the issues associated with the biofilm infections in the orthopedic field for which he developed and promulgated the concept of \"intelligent implants\"<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://scholarworks.montana.edu/xmlui/bitstream/handle/1/13392/04-005_Intelligent_Implants.pdf?sequence=1|title=Intelligent Implants to Battle Biofilms}}</ref> that would serve both their structural/dynamic functions while simultaneously self-monitoring and treating biofilm infections \"in situ\" as they occurred.<ref>{{Cite journal|title=Orthopaedic biofilm infections|first1=Paul|last1=Stoodley|first2=Garth D.|last2=Ehrlich|first3=Parish P.|last3=Sedghizadeh|first4=Luanne|last4=Hall-Stoodley|first5=Mark E.|last5=Baratz|first6=Daniel T.|last6=Altman|first7=Nicholas G.|last7=Sotereanos|first8=John William|last8=Costerton|first9=Patrick|last9=Demeo|date=November 12, 2011|journal=Current Orthopaedic Practice|volume=22|issue=6|pages=558–563|doi=10.1097/BCO.0b013e318230efcf|pmid=22323927|pmc=3272669}}</ref>\n\n===Clinical molecular diagnostics===\nOne of Ehrlich's earliest works used the polymerase chain reaction and focused on the detection of human retroviruses including, HTLV-I and HIV.<ref>{{Cite journal|url=https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S027227121830698X|title=Clinical and Molecular Parameters of HTLV-I Infection|first1=Garth D.|last1=Ehrlich|first2=Bernard J.|last2=Poiesz|date=March 1, 1988|journal=Clinics in Laboratory Medicine|volume=8|issue=1|pages=65–84|doi=10.1016/S0272-2712(18)30698-X|pmid=2896088 |url-access=subscription}}</ref> In these studies, he used [[nucleic acid]] amplification combined with oligonucleotide-detection procedures to diagnose HIV in the infected strains.<ref name=\"auto\"/> More recently, his work during the [[COVID-19 pandemic]] included the establishment of a molecular diagnostics facility that provided both detection and sequence-based typing for [[SARS-CoV-2]]. In addition, he collaborated with a group of physicians to develop appropriate precautionary measures, and recommendations regarded as essential prior to the resuming of the Elective Orthopaedic Surgery with a strict emphasis on preventing the spread of COVID-19.<ref>{{Cite journal|title=Resuming Elective Orthopaedic Surgery During the COVID-19 Pandemic: Guidelines Developed by the International Consensus Group (ICM)|first1=J.|last1=Parvizi|first2=T.|last2=Gehrke|first3=C. A.|last3=Krueger|first4=E.|last4=Chisari|first5=M.|last5=Citak|first6=S.|last6=Van Onsem|first7=W. L.|last7=Walter|date=July 15, 2020|journal=The Journal of Bone and Joint Surgery. American Volume|volume=102|issue=14|pages=1205–1212|doi=10.2106/JBJS.20.00844|pmid=32675662 |pmc=7431146 }}</ref>\n\nMuch of Ehrlich's clinical and [[translational research]] has focused on the integration of technologies in bacterial genomics which have led to the identification of pathogens etiologically associated with various pathogenic disease processes. Through his early adoption and development of species-specific pan-domain molecular diagnostics including the Ibis Biosciences T5000 and his lab-developed, long-read 16S microbiome assay he has played a role in demonstrating that many inflammatory conditions are actually chronic polymicrobial infections in which the pathogens remain largely unculturable.  In particular, he has used these approaches in the study of orthopedic, neurosurgical, and wound infections to identify both known and novel pathogens in multiple conditions including: periprosthetic 'sterile' loosenings;<ref>{{Cite journal|title=What role do periodontal pathogens play in osteoarthritis and periprosthetic joint infections of the knee?|first1=Garth D.|last1=Ehrlich|first2=Fen Z.|last2=Hu|first3=Nicholas|last3=Sotereanos|first4=Jeffrey|last4=Sewicke|first5=Javad|last5=Parvizi|first6=Peter L.|last6=Nara|first7=Carla Renata|last7=Arciola|date=January 4, 2014|journal=Journal of Applied Biomaterials & Functional Materials|volume=12|issue=1|pages=13–20|doi=10.5301/jabfm.5000203|pmid=24921460 }}{{Retracted|doi=10.5301/JABFM.2017.17646|pmid=29048705|http://retractionwatch.com/2018/01/24/terrible-experience-life-authorship-dispute-leads-lawsuit/ ''Retraction Watch''|intentional=yes}}</ref> suspected infected arthroplasties; 'aseptic' arthritis; and ventroperitoneal shunts<ref>{{Cite journal|title=Biofilm-related infections of cerebrospinal fluid shunts|first1=C. A.|last1=Fux|first2=M.|last2=Quigley|first3=A. M.|last3=Worel|first4=C.|last4=Post|first5=S.|last5=Zimmerli|first6=G.|last6=Ehrlich|first7=R. H.|last7=Veeh|date=April 4, 2006|journal=Clinical Microbiology and Infection|volume=12|issue=4|pages=331–337|doi=10.1111/j.1469-0691.2006.01361.x|pmid=16524409|doi-access=free}}</ref> as well as chronic non-healing wounds.<ref>{{Cite journal|title=Characterization of Bacterial Communities in Venous Insufficiency Wounds by Use of Conventional Culture and Molecular Diagnostic Methods - PMC|year=2011 |pmc=3209111 |last1=Tuttle |first1=M. S. |last2=Mostow |first2=E. |last3=Mukherjee |first3=P. |last4=Hu |first4=F. Z. |last5=Melton-Kreft |first5=R. |last6=Ehrlich |first6=G. D. |last7=Dowd |first7=S. E. |last8=Ghannoum |first8=M. A. |journal=Journal of Clinical Microbiology |volume=49 |issue=11 |pages=3812–3819 |doi=10.1128/JCM.00847-11 |pmid=21880958 }}</ref>\n\nGiven the increasing prevalence of [[tick-borne disease]]s, Ehrlich along with a team of researchers expanded their research into the pathogenesis, fundamental biology, and detection strategies, and conducted a large-scale sequencing among the Borreliaceae for the formulation of a family-level pan-genome to be used for the development of species-specific and strain-specific diagnostics.<ref>{{cite journal | doi=10.3390/genes13091604 | doi-access=free | title=Large-Scale Sequencing of Borreliaceae for the Construction of Pan-Genomic-Based Diagnostics | year=2022 | last1=Socarras | first1=Kayla M. | last2=Haslund-Gourley | first2=Benjamin S. | last3=Cramer | first3=Nicholas A. | last4=Comunale | first4=Mary Ann | last5=Marconi | first5=Richard T. | last6=Ehrlich | first6=Garth D. | journal=Genes | volume=13 | issue=9 | page=1604 | pmid=36140772 | pmc=9498496 }}</ref>\n\n===Gene mapping and cloning===\nEhrlich's research works have led to the mapping and cloning of multiple human disease genes (alleles) as we well as the identification and characterization of virulence genes within pathogens through his introduction of [[statistical genetics]] and machine-learning methods to the characterization of unannotated bacterial genes. Most notably he led the research that identified the genetic bases for several [[Craniofacial abnormality|craniofacial abnormalities]]<ref>{{Cite journal|url=https://www.nature.com/articles/ng0694-149|title=A gene for Crouzon craniofacial dysostosis maps to the long arm of chromosome 10|first1=Robert A.|last1=Preston|first2=J. Christopher|last2=Post|first3=Bronya J. B.|last3=Keats|first4=Christopher E.|last4=Aston|first5=Robert E.|last5=Ferrell|first6=Janice|last6=Priest|first7=Nassim|last7=Nouri|first8=H. Wolfgang|last8=Losken|first9=Colleen A.|last9=Morris|first10=Mark R.|last10=Hurtt|first11=John J.|last11=Mulvihill|first12=Garth D.|last12=Ehrlich|date=June 12, 1994|journal=Nature Genetics|volume=7|issue=2|pages=149–153|doi=10.1038/ng0694-149|pmid=7920632 |s2cid=24248252 |url-access=subscription}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url= https://www.nextprot.org/publication/667979#!|title= Crouzon syndrome: mutations in two spliceoforms of FGFR2 and a common point mutation shared with Jackson-Weiss syndrome.}}</ref> and hereditary pancreatitis;<ref>{{Cite journal|title=A gene for hereditary pancreatitis maps to chromosome 7q35|first1=D. C.|last1=Whitcomb|first2=R. A.|last2=Preston|first3=C. E.|last3=Aston|first4=M. J.|last4=Sossenheimer|first5=P. S.|last5=Barua|first6=Y.|last6=Zhang|first7=A.|last7=Wong-Chong|first8=G. J.|last8=White|first9=P. G.|last9=Wood|first10=L. K.|last10=Gates|first11=C.|last11=Ulrich|first12=S. P.|last12=Martin|first13=J. C.|last13=Post|first14=G. D.|last14=Ehrlich|date=June 12, 1996|journal=Gastroenterology|volume=110|issue=6|pages=1975–1980|doi=10.1053/gast.1996.v110.pm8964426|pmid=8964426|doi-access=free}}</ref> in which multiple mutations in the cationic trypsinogen gene were shown to be associated with the hereditary pancreatitis (HP).<ref>{{Cite journal|title=Hereditary pancreatitis is caused by a mutation in the cationic trypsinogen gene|first1=D. C.|last1=Whitcomb|first2=M. C.|last2=Gorry|first3=R. A.|last3=Preston|first4=W.|last4=Furey|first5=M. J.|last5=Sossenheimer|first6=C. D.|last6=Ulrich|first7=S. P.|last7=Martin|first8=L. K.|last8=Gates|first9=S. T.|last9=Amann|first10=P. P.|last10=Toskes|first11=R.|last11=Liddle|first12=K.|last12=McGrath|first13=G.|last13=Uomo|first14=J. C.|last14=Post|first15=G. D.|last15=Ehrlich|date=October 12, 1996|journal=Nature Genetics|volume=14|issue=2|pages=141–145|doi=10.1038/ng1096-141|pmid=8841182|s2cid=21974705 }}</ref> Moreover, the research findings on hereditary pancreatitis also illustrated that the mutation in cationic trypsinogen gene is associated with the recurrent acute and chronic pancreatitis, and argued that [[The Chronic|the chronic]] pancreatitis could be due to the recurrent pancreatitis.<ref>{{Cite journal|title=Mutations in the cationic trypsinogen gene are associated with recurrent acute and chronic pancreatitis|first1=M C|last1=Gorry|first2=D|last2=Gabbaizedeh|first3=W|last3=Furey|first4=L K|last4=Gates|first5=R A|last5=Preston|first6=C E|last6=Aston|first7=Y|last7=Zhang|first8=C|last8=Ulrich|first9=G D|last9=Ehrlich|first10=D C|last10=Whitcomb|date=October 1, 1997|journal=Gastroenterology|volume=113|issue=4|pages=1063–1068|doi=10.1053/gast.1997.v113.pm9322498|pmid=9322498|doi-access=free}}</ref>\n\nEhrlich's distributed genome hypothesis which predicted the bacterial pangenome led to his application of statistical genetics for the identification of bacterial distributed genes that are associated with virulence and tissue tropism. This approach led to his identification and characterization of ''Msf1'', an ''[[Haemophilus influenzae]]'' [[virulence factor]] that provides for the bacterium's survival and trafficking in human macrophages.<ref>{{Cite journal|title=Identification and Characterization of msf, a Novel Virulence Factor in Haemophilus influenzae|first1=Jennifer M.|last1=Kress-Bennett|first2=N. Luisa|last2=Hiller|first3=Rory A.|last3=Eutsey|first4=Evan|last4=Powell|first5=Mark J.|last5=Longwell|first6=Todd|last6=Hillman|first7=Tenisha|last7=Blackwell|first8=Barbara|last8=Byers|first9=Joshua C.|last9=Mell|first10=J. Christopher|last10=Post|first11=Fen Z.|last11=Hu|first12=Garth D.|last12=Ehrlich|first13=Benjamin A.|last13=Janto|date=December 12, 2016|journal=PLOS ONE|volume=11|issue=3|article-number=e0149891|doi=10.1371/journal.pone.0149891|pmid=26977929|pmc=4792463|bibcode=2016PLoSO..1149891K |doi-access=free }}</ref> In addition, he has investigated the bacterial evolution underlying bacterial adaptation to the human lung environment.<ref>{{Cite journal|url=https://orbit.dtu.dk/en/publications/deletion-and-acquisition-of-genomic-content-during-early-stage-ad|title=Deletion and acquisition of genomic content during early stage adaptation of Pseudomonas aeruginosa to a human host environment|first1=Martin H.|last1=Rau|first2=Rasmus Lykke|last2=Marvig|first3=Garth D.|last3=Ehrlich|first4=Søren|last4=Molin|first5=Lars|last5=Jelsbak|date=December 12, 2012|journal=Environmental Microbiology|volume=14|issue=8|pages=2200–2211|doi=10.1111/j.1462-2920.2012.02795.x|pmid=22672046 |bibcode=2012EnvMi..14.2200R |url-access=subscription}}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal|title=Antagonistic Pleiotropy in the Bifunctional Surface Protein FadL (OmpP1) during Adaptation of Haemophilus influenzae to Chronic Lung Infection Associated with Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease|first1=Javier|last1=Moleres|first2=Ariadna|last2=Fernández-Calvet|first3=Rachel L.|last3=Ehrlich|first4=Sara|last4=Martí|first5=Lucía|last5=Pérez-Regidor|first6=Begoña|last6=Euba|first7=Irene|last7=Rodríguez-Arce|first8=Sergey|last8=Balashov|first9=Ester|last9=Cuevas|first10=Josefina|last10=Liñares|first11=Carmen|last11=Ardanuy|first12=Sonsoles|last12=Martín-Santamaría|first13=Garth D.|last13=Ehrlich|first14=Joshua Chang|last14=Mell|first15=Junkal|last15=Garmendia|editor-first=Julian|editor-last=Parkhill|date=November 7, 2018|journal=mBio|volume=9|issue=5|pages=e01176–18|doi=10.1128/mBio.01176-18|doi-access=free|pmid=30254117|pmc=6156194}}</ref>\n\nEhrlich's recent research on the etiology of [[Alzheimer's disease]] (AD) is based on the argument that chronic biofilm infections contribute to the development of AD by continually triggering the brain's antimicrobial peptide response. This leads to an accumulation of the Abeta peptide and its polymeric form, beta-amyloid that is, at least in part, responsible for triggering the symptoms of dementia. According to his research, a complex polymicrobial dynamic contributes to the pathogenicity of Alzheimer.<ref>{{Cite journal|first1=Yves|last1=Moné|first2=Joshua P.|last2=Earl|first3=Jarosław E.|last3=Król|first4=Azad|last4=Ahmed|first5=Bhaswati|last5=Sen|first6=Garth D.|last6=Ehrlich|first7=Jeffrey R.|last7=Lapides|title=Evidence supportive of a bacterial component in the etiology for Alzheimer's disease and for a temporal-spatial development of a pathogenic microbiome in the brain |journal=Frontiers in Cellular and Infection Microbiology |date=2023 |volume=13 |article-number=1123228 |doi=10.3389/fcimb.2023.1123228 |doi-access=free |pmid=37780846 |biorxiv=10.1101/2022.08.28.505614}}</ref> Speaking with Katie Clark for the ''Drexel University Research Magazine'', he commented, \"Despite the billions that have been spent, we have no effective drugs, we have done nothing to change the natural history of the disease... The approach people are taking is not right.\"<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://exelmagazine.org/article/the-infection-connection/|title=The Infection Connection – Exel: Drexel University's Research Magazine|first=Katie|last=Clark}}</ref>\n\n==Awards and honors==\n*1995 — Alumni Citation Award, Alfred University<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://path.upmc.edu/showcase/Quarterly/1995-2qtr.html|title=The Pathology Quarterly|website=path.upmc.edu}}</ref>\n*2014 — Elected Fellow, American Association for the Advancement of Science\n*2017 — Established Investigator Award, Institute for Molecular Medicine & Infectious Disease<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://drexel.edu/medicine/about/departments/institute-for-molecular-medicine-infectious-disease/news/archive/|title=Institute for Molecular Medicine & Infectious Disease News Archive|date=April 25, 2022|website=College of Medicine}}</ref>\n*2022 — Elected Fellow, American Academy of Microbiology\n*2023 - Member, American Society of Microbiology Distinguished Lecture (ASMDL).  panelhttps://asm.org/press-releases/2024/september/asm-announces-2024-2025-distinguished-lecturer-asm\n*2025 - Elected Foundational Fellow, International Lyme and Associated Diseases Society. https://www.ilads.org/fellowship-program/\n\n==Bibliography==\n===Books===\n*''Culture-Negative Orthopedic Biofilm Infections''. Springer Verlag Series on Biofilms (2012) {{ISBN|978-3-642-29553-9}}\n*''PCR-Based Diagnostics in Infectious Disease'' (2004) {{ISBN|978-0-86542-252-0}}\n\n===Selected articles===\n*Whitcomb, D. C., Gorry, M. C., Preston, R. A., Furey, W., Sossenheimer, M. J., Ulrich, C. D., ... & Ehrlich, G. D. (1996). Hereditary pancreatitis is caused by a mutation in the cationic trypsinogen gene. Nature genetics, 14(2), 141–145.\n*Sauer, K., Camper, A. K., Ehrlich, G. D., Costerton, J. W., & Davies, D. G. (2002). Pseudomonas aeruginosa displays multiple phenotypes during development as a biofilm.\n*Costerton, W., Veeh, R., Shirtliff, M., Pasmore, M., Post, C., & Ehrlich, G. (2003). The application of biofilm science to the study and control of chronic bacterial infections. The Journal of clinical investigation, 112(10), 1466–1477.\n*Borriello, G., Werner, E., Roe, F., Kim, A. M., Ehrlich, G. D., & Stewart, P. S. (2004). Oxygen limitation contributes to antibiotic tolerance of Pseudomonas aeruginosa in biofilms. Antimicrobial agents and chemotherapy, 48(7), 2659–2664.\n*Hall-Stoodley, L., Hu, F. Z., Gieseke, A., Nistico, L., Nguyen, D., Hayes, J., ... & Kerschner, J. E. (2006). Direct detection of bacterial biofilms on the middle-ear mucosa of children with chronic otitis media. Jama, 296(2), 202–211.\n*Parvizi, J., Gehrke, T., Krueger, C. A., Chisari, E., Citak, M., Van Onsem, S., ... & Zhou, Y. (2020). Resuming elective orthopaedic surgery during the COVID-19 pandemic: guidelines developed by the International Consensus Group (ICM). The Journal of bone and joint surgery. American volume, 102(14), 1205.\n*Dupré, D. A., Cheng, B., Kreft, R., Nistico, L., Ehrlich, G. D., Averick, S., & Altman, D. T. (2022). The Presence of Biofilms in Instrumented Spinal Fusions. Genetic Testing and Molecular Biomarkers, 26(7–8), 375–381.\n\n==References==\n{{reflist}}\n\n{{authority control}}\n\n{{DEFAULTSORT:Ehrlich, Garth}}\n[[Category:Living people]]\n[[Category:Alfred University alumni]]\n[[Category:Syracuse University alumni]]\n[[Category:Drexel University faculty]]\n[[Category:American molecular biologists]]\n[[Category:Fellows of the American Association for the Advancement of Science]]\n[[Category:University of Pittsburgh faculty]]\n[[Category:21st-century American biologists]]\n[[Category:Scientific journal editors]]\n[[Category:Year of birth missing (living people)]]\n[[Category:American microbiologists]]"}}}],"contentmodel":"wikitext","pagelanguage":"en","pagelanguagehtmlcode":"en","pagelanguagedir":"ltr","touched":"2026-11-17T20:08:19Z","lastrevid":1379994366,"length":33292}]}}